The engine reuses the package-level language machinery
(\cref{ch:overview}) rather than inventing its own, so the whole game shares
one default and one fallback rule.  Importing [[WELCOME]] as well lets the
engine greet the player without restating wording already defined for the
package's own greeting.

<<imports>>=
from dataclasses import dataclass

from maventyr import DEFAULT_LANGUAGE, WELCOME, localize
@

\section{Commands: what the player wants to do}

A command is a single player intention --- look around, go north, answer a
question --- captured as data, with no trace of whether it arrived as a typed
line, a button press, or a script.  We model the family as a base class with
one frozen dataclass per intention, rather than a string verb plus a bag of
arguments, because each intention carries different data and the frontends
dispatch on it: matching on [[Look]] versus a future [[Go]] and binding their
fields is clearer and safer than switching on a string and indexing a dict.

Freezing the dataclasses makes commands hashable and compared by value, and
safe to keep in a log or an undo history --- a command records what was
asked, and nothing should mutate it after the fact.

<<classes>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Command:
    """Base class for a player's intention, independent of any frontend."""
@

The parser in a later chapter will produce these from text; for now we need
one concrete command to exercise the engine.  [[Look]] is the natural choice:
it asks only to perceive the surroundings, so it carries no data and stays
meaningful once there is a world to describe.

<<classes>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Look(Command):
    """Request a description of the player's current surroundings."""
@

\section{Events: what the engine reports}

An event is something the engine reports as having happened, again as data a
frontend renders however it likes.  Keeping events inert --- text to show,
not a call that shows it --- is exactly what lets one engine drive both a
terminal and a browser: the engine decides \emph{what} to say, each frontend
decides \emph{how}.

<<classes>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Event:
    """Base class for something the engine reports to the frontend."""
@

The one event we need now carries a line of prose for the player.  Later
chapters add richer events --- a move, a blocked door, a posed question ---
but each is just another frozen dataclass the frontends learn to render.

<<classes>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Message(Event):
    """A line of text for the player to read."""

    text: str
@

\section{The engine}

Before the engine itself we need one more localized string: the reply when
the player asks for something the engine cannot yet do.  We store it as a
constant directory, like every other user-facing string, so the
not-understood reply is translated in step with the rest of the game.

<<constants>>=
DONT_UNDERSTAND = {
    "sv": "Jag förstår inte vad du menar.",
    "en": "I don't understand what you mean.",
}
@

The engine holds two pieces of state, each a property of the running game
rather than of any single command: the \emph{world} it drives and the
\emph{language} it speaks.  The world is a [[Map]] (\cref{ch:maps}) ---
\emph{handed in} by the caller, never imported, which is the whole reason the
core stays free of any concrete world type and the dependency runs from the
world to the engine and never back.  The language is kept here for the same
\enquote{property of the game} reason: later the command-line interface reads
it from configuration and passes it in, and the engine localizes every event
it emits in that one language.  Both take a neutral default --- no world, the
package's default language --- so the contract tests below can construct a bare
engine, while the loop and the world-touching commands supply a real [[Map]].

<<classes>>=
class Engine:
    """Maps player commands to the events they produce.

    Constructed with the world it drives and the language it speaks. The
    engine touches no user interface: a caller passes a Command to ``handle``
    (or a script of input lines to ``run``) and renders the returned Events
    itself.
    """

    def __init__(self, world=None, language=DEFAULT_LANGUAGE):
        self.world = world
        self.language = language

    <<engine methods>>
@

\subsection{Handling a command}

[[handle]] is the whole command\,$\to$\,events contract in one method: a
command goes in, a list of events comes out.  We dispatch with a [[match]] and
keep the recognised cases in their own [[command cases]] bucket: a later
chapter adds a command by appending a [[case]] there, never by editing this
method --- the extension point the chapter promised --- and pattern matching
binds a command's fields in the same step that selects it.

<<engine methods>>=
def handle(self, command):
    """Return the list of events produced by ``command``."""
    match command:
        <<command cases>>
        case _:
            return [Message(localize(DONT_UNDERSTAND, self.language))]
@

The wildcard arm is deliberate.  A command whose type the engine does not yet
implement is not an error to crash on but a feature not built --- so the
engine replies, in the player's language, that it did not understand, and the
game plays on.  This is a different concern from the parser's job of rejecting
unparseable \emph{text}: by the time a command reaches [[handle]] it is already
a well-formed [[Command]]; the wildcard catches \emph{kinds} of command the
engine has no rule for yet, while the parser's own failure --- met further down
this chapter --- is a line that named no command at all.

The first recognised case is [[Look]].  It asks the world to describe where the
player stands, handing off to the [[Map]]'s own [[look]] (\cref{ch:maps}) rather
than phrasing anything itself: the engine decides \emph{that} the player looks,
the world knows \emph{what} they see.  This single line is the injected seam
made concrete --- [[handle]] names [[self.world.look]] without the module ever
importing [[Map]], so a reader sees exactly how little of the world the engine
depends on.

<<command cases>>=
case Look():
    return self.world.look(self.language)
@

To exercise the known-command path we need a world for the engine to look at,
but \emph{which} world is exactly what the seam hides.  So the tests supply a
stand-in that implements only the two methods the engine ever calls on a world
--- [[look]] and [[move]] --- and has each echo the language it was handed.
That this stub is enough to drive the engine is the architectural claim made
executable: the engine depends on this tiny interface, not on [[Map]].

<<test [[game.py]]>>=
import inspect

import maventyr.game
from maventyr.game import *


class _StubWorld:
    """A stand-in world exposing only what the engine calls on it.

    Each method echoes the language it is handed, so a test can check that the
    engine forwards its own ``language`` rather than a hard-coded default.
    """

    def look(self, language=DEFAULT_LANGUAGE):
        return [Message(f"omkring [{language}]")]

    def move(self, direction, language=DEFAULT_LANGUAGE):
        return [Message(f"mot {direction} [{language}]")]
@

A [[Look]] returns whatever the world describes, in the engine's language ---
Swedish by default and English when so configured, which proves the language is
a real seam carried through to the world rather than fixed in the engine:

<<test [[game.py]]>>=
def test_look_describes_the_world():
    world = _StubWorld()
    assert Engine(world).handle(Look()) == world.look("sv")


def test_look_is_localized_to_the_engines_language():
    world = _StubWorld()
    assert Engine(world, language="en").handle(Look()) == world.look("en")
@

An unhandled command falls through to the wildcard and yields the
not-understood reply, still localized; and whatever the command, [[handle]]
always returns a list of [[Event]]s, the shape every frontend relies on:

<<test [[game.py]]>>=
def test_unknown_command_is_not_understood():
    class Jump(Command):
        pass

    assert Engine().handle(Jump()) == [Message(DONT_UNDERSTAND["sv"])]


def test_handle_returns_a_list_of_events():
    events = Engine(_StubWorld()).handle(Look())
    assert isinstance(events, list)
    assert all(isinstance(event, Event) for event in events)
@

Finally, the rule that justifies this whole chapter: the core must touch no
user interface.  We assert it mechanically by reading the tangled module's
own source and checking that neither [[print]] nor [[input]] is called, so a
future change that smuggles I/O into the core fails the test suite rather than
a later architecture review:

<<test [[game.py]]>>=
def test_core_calls_no_print_or_input():
    source = inspect.getsource(maventyr.game)
    assert "print(" not in source
    assert "input(" not in source
@

\section{Parsing player input}

The engine speaks Commands, but a player at a terminal types a \emph{line of
text}.  Something must bridge the two, and that bridge is the parser: it reads a
line and returns the [[Command]] it names, so a frontend's whole input path is
[[parse]] then [[Engine.handle]].  Parsing is the counterpart of the rule that
keeps the core mute (\cref{ch:overview}) --- the core never reads the keyboard,
so a separate, testable function turns whatever the keyboard produced into the
core's own vocabulary, with no [[input]] call anywhere in sight.%
\ltnote{%
  We introduce the rest of the command family \emph{here}, beside the parser
  that produces them, rather than back in the Commands section with [[Look]].
  Each command earns its place the moment the verb table maps a word to it, so
  the reader meets [[Go]] when they meet \enquote{gå} --- the type and its
  reason to exist arrive together, at the parser's point of need.
}

\subsection{The rest of the command vocabulary}

Most verbs carry one piece of text: a direction to walk, an item to take, a
character to talk to, a thing to use, an answer to submit.  They share a shape
--- a single string field --- and differ only in what that string \emph{means},
which the field name records.  We give each its own type rather than a generic
[[Command(verb, arg)]] for the reason [[Look]] is already a type and not a
string: the engine matches on the kind and reads a named field, so a future
[[Go]] case binds [[direction]] in the same step that selects it.

<<classes>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Go(Command):
    """Move the player one step in a direction."""

    direction: str


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Take(Command):
    """Pick up a named item in the current location."""

    item: str


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Talk(Command):
    """Begin talking to a named character."""

    target: str


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Use(Command):
    """Use a named item."""

    item: str


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Answer(Command):
    """Submit ``text`` as the answer to a posed question."""

    text: str
@

The remaining verbs carry nothing: showing the map, saving, and quitting are
whole intentions on their own.  [[ShowMap]] takes that name rather than [[Map]]
to leave the labyrinth's [[Map]] type (\cref{ch:maps}) unambiguous --- one is a
request to draw, the other the world it asks about.

<<classes>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class ShowMap(Command):
    """Request a drawing of the discovered map."""


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Save(Command):
    """Request that the game be saved."""


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Quit(Command):
    """Request to leave the game."""
@

One command stands apart: the parser's answer when a line names no verb it
knows.  [[Unknown]] is not something the player asked for but something the
parser could not place, and it carries the original [[text]] so the engine can
react to it.  Routing failure through a command, rather than returning an event
straight from the parser, is what keeps every [[Event]] born in the engine: the
parser's one job stays \enquote{text in, [[Command]] out}, and the engine
remains the sole author of what the player is told.

<<classes>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Unknown(Command):
    """A line the parser could not turn into a known command.

    Carries the original ``text`` so the engine can answer with help.
    """

    text: str
@

\subsection{A registry of verbs}

To turn a word into a command we need a table from spelling to command.  It is
the mirror of the direction registry (\cref{ch:locations}): [[DIRECTIONS]] is
\emph{keyed} by a fixed token because the program looks a direction up by token,
whereas a verb is looked up \emph{by} the player's localized word, which is not
fixed, so the verb table is a list to scan rather than a dict to index.  Each
entry pairs the spellings a verb may take in each language with the command to
build and whether it consumes the rest of the line as an argument.

<<classes>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class VerbSpec:
    """One verb: its spellings, the command it builds, and its arity.

    ``words`` is a ``{language: [spelling, ...]}`` directory; ``command`` is
    the Command subclass to construct; ``takes_argument`` says whether the
    rest of the line is passed to that constructor.
    """

    words: dict
    command: type
    takes_argument: bool = False

    def spellings(self, language=DEFAULT_LANGUAGE):
        """Return this verb's casefolded spellings in ``language``."""
        return [word.casefold() for word in localize(self.words, language)]
@

The registry seeds one entry per verb the game understands, ordered as a help
message would list them.  The argument-taking verbs (\enquote{gå},
\enquote{ta}, \dots) pass the rest of the line on; the bare verbs
(\enquote{karta}, \enquote{spara}, \enquote{sluta}) take nothing.  Swedish
leads, with an English spelling beside it and room for synonyms ---
\enquote{titta} or \enquote{se} for looking --- because this table is the one
place a new word for an action is taught, exactly as [[DIRECTIONS]] is the one
place a new direction is.

<<verb registry>>=
VERBS = [
    VerbSpec({"sv": ["gå"], "en": ["go"]}, Go, takes_argument=True),
    VerbSpec({"sv": ["titta", "se"], "en": ["look"]}, Look),
    VerbSpec({"sv": ["ta"], "en": ["take"]}, Take, takes_argument=True),
    VerbSpec({"sv": ["prata"], "en": ["talk"]}, Talk, takes_argument=True),
    VerbSpec({"sv": ["använd"], "en": ["use"]}, Use, takes_argument=True),
    VerbSpec({"sv": ["svara"], "en": ["answer"]}, Answer, takes_argument=True),
    VerbSpec({"sv": ["karta"], "en": ["map"]}, ShowMap),
    VerbSpec({"sv": ["spara"], "en": ["save"]}, Save),
    VerbSpec({"sv": ["sluta"], "en": ["quit"]}, Quit),
]
@

\subsection{Parsing a line}

Parsing is deliberately shallow: the first word is the verb, the rest is its
argument.  We casefold the verb so that \enquote{Gå} and \enquote{gå} are the
same word, scan the registry for a spec that lists it in the active language,
and build that spec's command --- handing over the rest of the line when the
verb takes one.  Three inputs yield [[Unknown]]: an empty line, a word no spec
claims, and an argument-taking verb with nothing after it (a bare \enquote{ta}
asks to take \dots\ what?).  Because the lookup runs through [[localize]], an
unknown language still finds the Swedish spellings rather than failing.

<<functions>>=
def parse(text, language=DEFAULT_LANGUAGE):
    """Turn a line of player ``text`` into a Command.

    Reads the first word as the verb (in ``language``) and passes the rest of
    the line as its argument when the verb takes one.  Returns ``Unknown`` for
    an empty line, an unrecognised verb, or an argument-taking verb given no
    argument.
    """
    tokens = text.split()
    if not tokens:
        return Unknown(text)
    verb, *rest = tokens
    verb = verb.casefold()
    for spec in VERBS:
        if verb in spec.spellings(language):
            argument = " ".join(rest)
            if not spec.takes_argument:
                return spec.command()
            return spec.command(argument) if argument else Unknown(text)
    return Unknown(text)
@

Let us check the two arities.  A bare verb becomes its command, and the rest of
the line --- however many words --- becomes one argument, so a multi-word item
name or a spaced-out equation survives intact:

<<test [[game.py]]>>=
def test_parses_a_verb_with_no_argument():
    assert parse("titta") == Look()
    assert parse("karta") == ShowMap()
    assert parse("spara") == Save()
    assert parse("sluta") == Quit()


def test_parses_a_verb_with_its_argument():
    assert parse("gå norr") == Go("norr")
    assert parse("ta rostig nyckel") == Take("rostig nyckel")
    assert parse("svara x = 5") == Answer("x = 5")
@

The verb is read in the player's language and case-insensitively, and an
unknown language falls back to the Swedish spellings rather than failing:

<<test [[game.py]]>>=
def test_parses_in_the_configured_language():
    assert parse("go north", "en") == Go("north")
    assert parse("look", "en") == Look()


def test_verb_is_case_insensitive():
    assert parse("TITTA") == Look()


def test_unknown_language_falls_back_to_default_spellings():
    assert parse("gå norr", "xx") == parse("gå norr", "sv")
@

Unparseable input --- nothing, an unknown verb, or a verb missing its argument
--- becomes [[Unknown]] carrying the original line, never an exception:

<<test [[game.py]]>>=
def test_unparseable_input_becomes_unknown():
    assert parse("") == Unknown("")
    assert parse("dansa") == Unknown("dansa")
    assert parse("ta") == Unknown("ta")
@

\subsection{A helpful reply to input it cannot place}

An [[Unknown]] command reaching the engine is the parser saying \enquote{this
line named no verb I know}.  The engine answers not with the terse
not-understood line --- that one is reserved for a valid command kind it cannot
yet act on --- but with help: the verbs the player \emph{could} have used, in
their language.  [[known_verbs]] builds that list from the registry, taking each
verb's first spelling so the player sees one canonical word per action rather
than every synonym.

<<functions>>=
def known_verbs(language=DEFAULT_LANGUAGE):
    """Return the known verbs in ``language`` as a comma-separated string."""
    return ", ".join(spec.spellings(language)[0] for spec in VERBS)
@

<<constants>>=
HELP = {
    "sv": "Jag förstår inte. Försök med: {verbs}.",
    "en": "I don't understand. Try: {verbs}.",
}
@

The engine gains one case for it, beside the [[Look]] case in the same bucket
and so ahead of the wildcard, so a misparse is met with guidance while a valid
but unimplemented command still falls through to the neutral reply:

<<command cases>>=
case Unknown():
    return [
        Message(
            localize(HELP, self.language).format(
                verbs=known_verbs(self.language)
            )
        )
    ]
@

The reply lists the verbs and is localized: the same misparse is answered in
Swedish by default and in English when the engine is configured for it, and
either way it is a single [[Message]] naming a verb the player can actually
try:

<<test [[game.py]]>>=
def test_unknown_command_is_answered_with_localized_help():
    [reply] = Engine().handle(Unknown("dansa"))
    assert isinstance(reply, Message)
    assert "gå" in reply.text

    [english] = Engine(language="en").handle(Unknown("dansa"))
    assert "go" in english.text
    assert english.text != reply.text
@

\section{Driving the game}

Everything so far handles \emph{one} command.  A game is the loop that does so
turn after turn: show the player the room, read the line they type, report what
happened, and repeat until they leave.  Putting that loop \emph{here}, in the
core, is the one rule paying off a second time (\cref{ch:overview}).  The
control flow --- greet once, describe the room, parse each line, dispatch it,
stop on quit --- is game logic, not presentation, so it belongs where it can be
tested without a screen; the terminal frontend and the command-line entry point
of later chapters then shrink to the one thing only they can do, doing the
input and output the core refuses to.  The loop reads no keyboard and prints
nothing: it takes the lines as an argument and returns the events as a list.

Returning the whole transcript only at the end makes [[run]] a \emph{batch}
driver: it fits a scripted test, or any caller that already has every line in
hand, but not an interactive terminal, which must show each turn before it can
read the next.  That frontend reuses the loop's per-turn core --- [[parse]] then
[[handle]] --- and renders between the two; the control flow is the same one
proven here, only the moment of rendering moves.  So this chapter delivers the
logic of play once, and the interactive loop of a later chapter is a thin
re-timing of it rather than a second implementation.

\subsection{The two commands the loop still needs}

The loop leans on two commands the engine has not yet handled, and they have
waited for this section on purpose.%
\ltnote{%
  [[Look]]'s case sits up in the engine section, but [[Go]] and [[Quit]]'s wait
  until here, beside the loop that needs them.  The early section shows the
  dispatch \emph{shape} with the one command the reader had met; adding the rest
  there would force the [[Go]] and [[Quit]] types ahead of the parser that
  motivates them.  A case earns its place where its need is felt --- just as the
  parser introduced each verb where the verb table first mapped a word to it.
}
[[Go]] is the other half of movement: where [[Look]] asks the world to describe
the current room, [[Go]] asks it to \emph{walk} an exit.  The [[Map]] already
does that work --- resolving the typed direction to an exit token and returning
the move's events (\cref{ch:maps}) --- so the engine only forwards the word the
player typed and its own language, adding nothing.  In particular it does not
resolve \enquote{north} or \enquote{norrut} to a token: that is the world's job,
and duplicating it here would be a second place to keep in step.

<<command cases>>=
case Go(direction):
    return self.world.move(direction, self.language)
@

[[Quit]] is the loop's off switch.  Leaving is a fact about the session, not the
labyrinth, so the command touches no world; the engine answers with a localized
farewell and the loop, recognising the command it has just parsed, stops after
this turn.  Routing even the goodbye through an [[Event]] upholds the rule that
every line the player reads is born in the engine.  The farewell is a constant
directory beside its one use, like every other user-facing string:

<<constants>>=
FAREWELL = {
    "sv": "Hej då!",
    "en": "Goodbye!",
}
@

<<command cases>>=
case Quit():
    return [Message(localize(FAREWELL, self.language))]
@

\subsection{The loop}

[[run]] is the headless heart.  It greets the player once, shows the room they
begin in, then drives each line down the full input path --- [[parse]] to a
command, [[handle]] to its events --- gathering every event into one transcript
it returns.  The greeting lives here, not in [[Look]], because it is said once
on entering the game rather than each time the player looks around.  The opening
description, though, \emph{is} a look, so the loop issues the same [[Look]] the
player can rather than calling the world a second way: the [[Look]] case stays
the one place that maps looking to the world, which keeps the two in step when a
later chapter makes looking do more --- discovering the room, say
(\cref{ch:overview}).  Parsing happens inside the loop, at the input boundary,
so the loop has the command in hand and can stop the instant it is a [[Quit]]:
the player's own \enquote{sluta} ends the game, and any line typed after it is
never read.

<<engine methods>>=
def run(self, lines):
    """Drive a headless game over ``lines``, returning the transcript.

    Greets the player, describes the starting location, then parses and
    handles each line of input in turn, collecting every event, until a
    ``Quit`` command (or the lines run out). Reads no input and prints
    nothing: a frontend supplies the lines and renders the returned events.
    """
    events = [Message(localize(WELCOME, self.language)), *self.handle(Look())]
    for line in lines:
        command = parse(line, self.language)
        events += self.handle(command)
        if isinstance(command, Quit):
            break
    return events
@

Let us check the two new cases, then the whole loop.  A [[Go]] hands the typed
direction and the engine's language straight to the world, adding nothing of its
own:

<<test [[game.py]]>>=
def test_go_walks_the_world_in_the_engines_language():
    world = _StubWorld()
    engine = Engine(world, language="en")
    assert engine.handle(Go("north")) == world.move("north", "en")
@

A [[Quit]] is answered with the farewell, localized like every other reply, and
without needing a world at all:

<<test [[game.py]]>>=
def test_quit_is_answered_with_a_localized_farewell():
    assert Engine().handle(Quit()) == [Message(FAREWELL["sv"])]
    assert Engine(language="en").handle(Quit()) == [Message(FAREWELL["en"])]
@

Finally the acceptance: a scripted sequence of lines drives a whole game with no
user interface in sight.  The transcript opens with the welcome and the starting
room, then carries each command's events in order and ends with the farewell ---
